  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Monday) \nIssued at 1029 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nThe upper level ridge and associated area of high pressure centered \nnear the TX/NM border will amplify through mid-week as an omega \nblocking pattern becomes established. While a closed low digs \nsouthward from the PacNW towards the SoCal region. Increasing \nheights and thickness values from southwesterly flow aloft and lee \ntroughing will continue to support a gradual warming trend. Highs \nboth Wednesday and Thursday are expected to be the warmest of the \nweek, reaching back to near or slightly above seasonal normals. Dry \nconditions will also persist through mid-week as the upper ridge \nremains in place and provides subsidence, limiting forcing for \nascent and keeping precipitation low. \n\nHeading into the weekend, the pattern becomes more active as the \nupper trough begins to eject northeastward towards the High Plains. \nBoth the deterministic GFS and ECMWF are now in agreement compared \nto what we saw 24 hours ago, in regards of a secondary low near the \nbase of the parent trough. Though differences persist in regards to \nthe timing and evolution of this secondary low as it tracks eastward. \nDespite this uncertainty from global models, ensemble guidance \ncontinues to hint at the potential for beneficial rainfall across \nthe FA, with ensemble PWATs well above the 90th percentile for late \nOctober. Decent moisture return is expected to develop ahead of this \nsystem, aided by southwest flow aloft and an upslope component to \nthe surface winds helping to draw gulf moisture northward into the \nregion. Given the remaining spread in the overall evolution of the \ntrough, will continue to monitor trends in the coming days before \nbecoming more certain in timing and evolution. For now, will \nmaintain NBM mentionable PoPs area-wide. With this, not only may we \nsee our next best chance for rainfall, but can expect the return of \nfall-like temperatures.",
